To find the quotient when $x^5 + 7$ is divided by $x + 1$, we can use polynomial long division. We set up the division as follows:
$$
\begin{array}{r|l}
x + 1 & x^5 + 0x^4 + 0x^3 + 0x^2 + 0x + 7 \\
\cline{2-2}
 & x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1 \\
 & -(x^5 + x^4) \\
\cline{2-2}
 & -x^4 + 0x^3 + 0x^2 + 0x + 7 \\
 & -(-x^4 - x^3) \\
\cline{2-2}
 & x^3 + 0x^2 + 0x + 7 \\
 & -(x^3 + x^2) \\
\cline{2-2}
 & -x^2 + 0x + 7 \\
 & -(-x^2 - x) \\
\cline{2-2}
 & x + 0 + 7 \\
 & -(x + 1) \\
\cline{2-2}
 & -1 + 7 \\
\end{array}
$$
Thus, the quotient is $x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1$ with a remainder of $6.$ </think> <answer>6</answer>

